<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<p>Healthcare data is some of the hardest data to get right. It arrives from EHRs, labs, claims, devices and research systems, in different formats and code systems, with strict privacy obligations attached. I work with health systems, digital health companies, life-sciences teams and research groups who need that data to be usable.</p>
<p>Common situations:</p>
<ul>
<li>Every new integration is a bespoke project, and the backlog keeps growing.</li>
<li>Researchers spend months assembling cohorts that should take days.</li>
<li>A clinical AI idea is blocked because nobody can vouch for the data underneath it.</li>
<li>Security reviews stall every new use of patient data.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>The problems I solve</h2>
<h3>Interoperability</h3>
<p>I standardise exchange on HL7 FHIR, with adapters for legacy HL7 v2 feeds, so systems talk through one well-defined interface instead of dozens of point-to-point connections.</p>
<h3>Consistent clinical meaning</h3>
<p>Local codes are normalised to SNOMED CT, LOINC and RxNorm through a terminology service, so a diagnosis or a lab result means the same thing everywhere it is used. This is applied <a href="/ontology-knowledge-graph/">ontology work</a> with a large head start.</p>
<h3>Research-ready data</h3>
<p>For analytics and real-world evidence I land data in the OMOP common data model and use OHDSI tools such as ATLAS for cohort definitions that are reproducible and shareable. I explain the full pipeline in <a href="/insights/fhir-to-omop-architecture/">FHIR to OMOP: a practical architecture</a>.</p>
<h3>Privacy and compliance</h3>
<p>De-identification, consent management, encryption, fine-grained access control and audit trails are built in from the start, not added after a security review fails.</p>
<h3>Clinical AI you can defend</h3>
<p>Retrieval and agents grounded in clinical vocabularies and governed data, with provenance on every answer and human review where it matters. See my wider <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture</a> approach.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>My approach</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inventory sources, codes and PHI flows.</strong> Know exactly what data exists, where it moves and who can see it.</li>
<li><strong>Design the target architecture.</strong> FHIR for exchange, a terminology service for meaning, OMOP for analytics, governance across every hop.</li>
<li><strong>Prove it on one source and one use case.</strong> A single feed through to a working cohort or dashboard.</li>
<li><strong>Scale source by source.</strong> With data quality checks and mapping reviews at each step.</li>
<li><strong>Layer AI on governed data.</strong> Only once provenance and access control are in place.</li>
<li><strong>Train the team and hand over.</strong> Mappings, pipelines and runbooks owned by your people.</li>
</ol>
</section>
