<section class="block">
<h2>How an engagement runs</h2>
<p>Most healthcare work starts with a two to four week architecture review covering sources, standards, privacy and the target platform. That leads into a build-and-hand-off project delivered one source and one use case at a time. Where the organisation needs ongoing technical leadership, I continue as a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a>. If you operate in the Gulf, see my <a href="/uae/">UAE practice</a>, where residency and local data-protection rules apply as well.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>The <a href="/#work">clinical interoperability case study</a> shows a typical FHIR to OMOP engagement end to end.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Common pitfalls in healthcare data projects</h2>
<h3>Mapping too late</h3>
<p>Teams often load raw data first and plan to standardise later. Later rarely comes, and every downstream report carries its own local interpretation of the codes. I bring vocabulary mapping into the pipeline from the first source.</p>
<h3>One model for every purpose</h3>
<p>FHIR is excellent for exchanging data about a patient right now. It is a poor shape for population-level research. OMOP is the reverse. Trying to force one standard to do both jobs leads to slow queries or brittle integrations. Using each where it fits avoids both.</p>
<h3>Treating de-identification as a one-off</h3>
<p>De-identification is a pipeline step, not a project. New sources, new fields and free-text notes keep introducing identifiers. I design it as a repeatable, tested stage with clear rules for what is removed, generalised or tokenised, and audit evidence for each run.</p>
<h3>AI before provenance</h3>
<p>A clinical model trained or grounded on data nobody can trace is a liability. Lineage from source record to answer is what lets clinicians, researchers and auditors trust the output. I put provenance in place before any AI goes near patient data.</p>
<h3>Underestimating vocabulary maintenance</h3>
<p>Standard vocabularies are updated regularly. Mappings that were right last year can drift. A small, scheduled process to refresh vocabularies and review unmapped codes keeps the platform honest.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Questions to ask before you start</h2>
<ul>
<li>Which decisions should the platform support first: care coordination, quality reporting, research cohorts or AI?</li>
<li>Which sources are in scope, and who owns each one?</li>
<li>What consent and data-sharing agreements already exist, and what do they allow?</li>
<li>Where must the data physically live, and which cloud services are covered by your agreements?</li>
<li>Who will own mappings and data quality once the project ends?</li>
</ul>
<p>Clear answers to these five questions save months. If some are open, the architecture review is where we settle them, with your clinical, legal and IT leads in the room.</p>
</section>
